      <p>If you are primarily a subscriber who borrows from Kindle+'s catalog, the EPUB sideloading change is a nice bonus. If you maintain a large DRM-free personal library, a <a href="/blog/best-ereader-2026-expert-roundup/">dedicated EPUB reader</a> with open filesystem access is still the cleaner path — especially one running <a href="https://koreader.rocks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KOReader</a> or similar open-source firmware.</p>

      <h2>Troubleshooting Common Kindle+ EPUB Issues</h2>

      <p>Even with the improved pipeline, some EPUBs fail to deliver or render incorrectly. The most common culprits:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Corrupt OPF manifest.</strong> If the EPUB's package document references missing files, Amazon's validator rejects the entire archive. Open the EPUB in <a href="https://sigil-ebook.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sigil</a> and run the built-in EPUB check to locate the broken reference.</li>
        <li><strong>Embedded font licensing.</strong> Some EPUBs embed fonts with restrictive license flags that the KPF converter strips, causing fallback fonts to substitute. This is cosmetic, not fatal.</li>
        <li><strong>Large image payloads.</strong> EPUBs with uncompressed PNG or TIFF images can exceed the 200 MB ceiling. Recompress images to JPEG or WebP before uploading — our <a href="/blog/calibre-edit-epub-metadata-2026/">Calibre metadata guide</a> covers batch image optimization.</li>
        <li><strong>Region-locked accounts.</strong> Kindle+ EPUB delivery is currently limited to Amazon.com, Amazon.co.uk, Amazon.de, and Amazon.co.jp accounts. Other marketplaces still route through the older MOBI pipeline.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

      <p>Kindle+ finally brings Amazon's hardware in line with the rest of the ebook industry on format support, and for casual readers who buy most of their books from Amazon anyway, the EPUB addition removes the last friction point that pushed some users toward <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo</a> or PocketBook hardware. But the conversion to KPF, the DRM restrictions, and the loss of advanced EPUB 3 features mean that Kindle+ is still a walled garden — just one with a slightly wider gate.</p>

      <p>If your workflow involves converting between formats regularly — whether EPUB to Kindle, Kindle to EPUB, or anything in between — a dedicated converter tool will save you more time than any single-platform feature. That is the gap <a href="/">Converter EPUB</a> fills: browser-based, no upload to external servers, and support for every major ebook format.</p>

